See the file COPYING for more information.

Description:
------------
This plugin integrates SAT>IP network devices seamlessly into VDR.
You can use any SAT>IP channel like any other normal DVB channel for
live viewing, recording, etc. The plugin also features full section
filtering capabilities which allow for example EIT information to be
extracted from the incoming stream.

Requirements:
-------------
- Libcurl >= 7.36.0 - the multiprotocol file transfer library with RTSP support
  http://curl.haxx.se/libcurl/

- PugiXML - Light-weight, simple and fast XML parser for C++
  http://pugixml.org/
  or
  TinyXML2 - a simple, small, C++ XML parser
  https://leethomason.github.io/tinyxml2/

- Glibc >= 2.12 - the GNU C library (recvmmsg)
  http://www.gnu.org/software/libc/

- VDR >= 2.6.8


Installation:
-------------
tar -xzf /put/your/path/here/vdr-plugin-satip-X.Y.Z.tgz
make -C vdr-plugin-satip-X.Y.Z install

Configuration:
--------------

Command line parameters:
------------------------
  -d NUM,   --devices=NUM  Define how many VDR devices are created by the plugin and
                           therefore how many transponders can be received simultaneously
                           (default: 2)

  -s SERVER[;SERVER ....], --server=SERVER[;SERVER ....]
                           Manually configure one or more static SAT>IP server(s) if autodetection
                           via UPnP can't be used or is not desired
                           Multiple SERVER definitions can be given separated by a semicolon.

                           The SERVER definition has the following format:
                           [<srcaddress>@]<ipaddress>[:<port>]|<model>[:<filter>]|<description>[:<quirk>]

          <srcaddress> (optional)   Source address can be utilized to define the
                                    networking interface on the host to use, e.g. 127.0.0.1

          <ipaddress>               IP address of the SAT>IP server, e.g. 127.0.0.1

          <port>  (optional)        IP port number for RTSP of the SAT>IP server (default: 554)

          <model>                   Model defines the DVB delivery system (DVBS2,
                                    DVBT2, DVBT, DVBC) and number of frontends,
                                    separated by a hyphen, e.g. DVBS2-4;
                                    multiple models can be given separated by comma,
                                    e.g. DVBS2-4,DVBC-2

          <filter> (optional)       Filter can be used to limit satellite frontends
                                    to a certain satellite position, e.g. S19.2E;
                                    multiple filters can be given separated by comma,
                                    e.g. S19.2E,S13E

          <description>             Friendly name of SAT>IP server; this is used
                                    for autodetection of quirks

          <quirk> (optional)        Quirks are non-standard compliant features and
                                    workarounds for SAT>IP server defined by a
                                    hexadecimal number; multiple quirks can be
                                    defined by adding the values:

                                      0x01: Fix session ID bug
                                      0x02: Fix play parameter (addpids/delpids) bug
                                      0x04: Fix frontend locking bug
                                      0x08: Support for RTP over TCP
                                      0x10: Support the X_PMT protocol extension
                                      0x20: Support the CI TNR protocol extension
                                      0x40: Fix auto-detection of pilot tones bug
                                      0x80: Fix re-tuning bug by teardowning a session

          Examples:
            vdr -P 'satip -s 192.168.0.1|DVBS2-2,DVBT2-2|OctopusNet'
            vdr -P 'satip -s 192.168.0.1|DVBS2-4|OctopusNet;192.168.0.2|DVBT2-4|minisatip:0x18'
            vdr -P 'satip -s 192.168.0.1:554|DVBS2-2:S19.2E|OctopusNet;192.168.0.2:8554|DVBS2-4:S19.2E,S1W|minisatip'

  -c CA_IDS, --caids=CA_IDS
                           To use the CI slot(s) of the OctopusNet define the CA IDs
                           supported by each CI slot; multiple CA IDs per slot can be given
                           separated by comma, multiple slots are separated by semicolon;
                           the CA_IDS are proivided in the following format:
                           <CAM1_ID1>[,<CAM1_ID2,...][;<CAM2_ID1>[,<CAM2_ID2>,...];....]

                           Example: CA ID 0x1000 in slot 1, CA IDs 0x2000 and 0x2020 in slot 2:
                           --caids=0x1000;0x2000,0x2020

                           Additionally in the plugin's settings you need to enable the
                           "Use CI extension" and define the VDR device(s) the CI slot(s)
                           will be attached to; Up to 4 CAMs are supported

  -p START-END, --portrange=START-END
                           Specify manually the RTP & RTCP port range by START and END which
                           enables using the plugin through NAT (e.g. Docker bridged network);
                           a minimum of 2 ports per device is required

  -D,       --detach       start the plugin in detached mode; this disables
                           all devices which can be anabled later with the SVDRP ATTA command

  -S,       --single       enable the single model server mode;
                           this creates multiple logical SAT>IP servers when used
                           with multiple models (delivery systems) in the SERVER parameter

  -n,       --noquirks     disable autodetection of server quirks

  -N,       --nodisconnect disable disconnecting idle streams from the SAT>IP server;
                           this can be used to keep a connection and therefore "reserve"
                           a frontend on the SAT>IP server for the SATIP plugin

  -t MODE,  --trace=MODE   set the tracing mode MODE; see log.h in the source code for details

  -r SIZE,  --rcvbuf=SIZE  override the size of the RTP receive buffer with SIZE in bytes


Special Configurations:
-----------------------
SAT>IP satellite positions (aka. signal sources) shall be defined via
VDR's sources.conf. If the source description begins with a number, it's used
as SAT>IP signal source selection parameter. A special number zero can
be used to disable the source. Otherwise, the default parameter is one:

S19.2E  Astra 1KR/1L/1M/2C
=> Signal source = 1

S19.2E  2
=> Signal source = 2

S19.2E  3 Astra 1KR/1L/1M/2C
=> Signal source = 3

S19.2E 0 Astra 1KR/1L/1M/2C
=> Source is disabled

A channel can be assigned into a specific SAT>IP frontend by giving the
identifier number in RID field of a channels.conf entry:
FE = RID % 100
Valid range: 1 ... 99


Setup menu:
-----------
  - Operating mode = low            Selects the priority of SAT>IP devices:
                                      off:    exclude all SAT>IP devices from VDR's device handling
                                              which means no SAT>IP devices will be used
                                      low:    prefer other available devices like local DVB cards
                                      normal: SAT>IP devices have same priority than other devices
                                      hight:  prefer SAT>IP devices over other available devices

  - Use CI extension = no           If you want to use the CI extension found
                                    in some SAT>IP hardware (e.g. Digital
                                    Devices OctopusNet), set this option to "yes".

  -  VDR Device # assigned CI/CAM   if "Use CI extension" is enabled you need to
                                    assign here a CI/CAM to a VDR Device

  -   supported CA IDs of CI/CAM #  readonly, displays the configured CA IDs of
                                    the CI slot above if a CI/CAM is assinged

  - Enable EPG scanning = yes       If you want exclude all SAT>IP devices
                                    from VDR's EIT background scanning for EPG
                                    set this option to "no".

  - Disabled sources = none         If your SAT>IP servers don't have certain
                                    satellite positions available you can
                                    disable them via this option. If this option
                                    is set to a non-zero value, the menu page will
                                    contain that many "Disabled sources" options
                                    which allow you to disable the individual sources
                                    Valid range: 0 (= "none") ... 25

  - Disabled filters = none         Certain section filters might cause some
                                    unwanted behaviour to VDR such as time
                                    being falsely synchronized. This option
                                    allows creation of blacklists of ill-behaving
                                    filters. If this option is set to a non-zero
                                    value, the menu page will contain that many
                                    "Disable filters" options which allow you
                                    to disable the individual section filters.
                                    Valid range: 0 (= "none") ... 5

  - Transport mode = unicast        If you want to use the non-standard
                     multicast      RTP-over-TCP transport mode, set this option
                     rtp-o-tcp      accordingly. Otherwise, the transport
                                    mode will be RTP-over-UDP via unicast or
                                    multicast.

  - Enable frontend reuse = yes     Certain devices might have artifacts if
                                    multiple channels are assigned to the same
                                    frontend. If you want to avoid such a
                                    frontend assignment, set this option to "no".
                                    For receiving multiple channels of one
                                    transponder with one CI/CAM you need to set
                                    this option to "yes".
  Buttons:
  - [Red Button:Scan]               Forces network scanning of SAT>IP hardware.
  - [Yellow Button:Devices]         Opens SAT>IP device status menu.
  - [Blue Button:Info]              Opens SAT>IP information/statistics menu.
  - [Ok]                            Opens information menu of selected SAT>IP
                                    device.

Information menu:
  Buttons:
  - [Red Button:General]            Opens the general information page.
  - [Green Button:Pids]             Opens the pid statistics page.
  - [Yellow Button:Filters]         Opens the section filter statistics page.
  - [Blue Button:Bits/bytes]        Toggles between bits and bytes mode.


SVDRP commands:
---------------
The plugin provides several SVDRP commands which can be executed
e.g. by "svdrpsend PLUG satip <command> [<parameters>]"

  INFO [ <page> ] [ <card index> ]
          Prints SAT>IP device information and statistics
          The output can be narrowed using the  "page" option:
            1 = general
            2 = pids
            3 = section filters

  MODE    Toggles between bit or byte information mode

  LIST    Lists active SAT>IP servers

  SCAN    Scans active SAT>IP servers

  STAT    Lists status information of SAT>IP devices

  CONT    Shows SAT>IP device count

  OPER [ off | low | normal | high ]
          Get or set operating mode of SAT>IP devices

  ATTA    Attach active SAT>IP servers

  DETA    Detachs active SAT>IP servers

  TRAC [ <mode> ]
          Get or set tracing mode flags
          See log.h in the source code for details


Notes:
------
- If you are having problems receiving DVB-S2 channels, make sure your
  channels.conf entry contains correct pilot tone setting.

- The stream id "-1" states about unsuccessful tuning. This might be a
  result of invalid channel parameters or lack of free SAT>IP tuners.

- If the plugin doesn't detect your SAT>IP network device, make sure
  your setup doesn't have firewalled the UDP port 1900.

- Stream decryption requires a separate CAM plugin that works without
  direct access to any DVB card devices. Also the integrated CAM slots
  in Octopus Net devices are supported.

- Tracing can be set on/off dynamically via command-line switch or
  SVDRP command.

- For OctopusNet firmware version 2.0 or greater is recommended.

- Inverto OEM firmware 1.17.0.120 or greater recommended.
  The firmware 1.16.0.120 can be downloaded and installed
  from their webpage: http://www.inverto.tv/support/
  An update to a newer firmware should be offered afterwards.

- FRITZ!OS 7.00 or greater recommended for FRITZ!Box Cable devices.
  Older firmware versions require both PlayPids and ForceLock quirks.

- If you are experiencing glitches in the video stream, one possible
  reason can be buffer overflows in RTP receive sockets. You can verify
  this by checking "receive buffer errors" counter by running "netstat -s"
  command. If the counter increases every time a video glitch happens,
  you should try to tweak the RTP receive buffer size with the "--rcvbuf"
  (-r) plugin parameter.
  A good starting point for the buffer size is to double the operating
  system default value until errors disappear or the maximum value is
  reached. You can check these values in Linux by checking the kernel
  parameters:
  $ cat /proc/sys/net/core/rmem_default
  $ cat /proc/sys/net/core/rmem_max
